<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/advanced/economic-history">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Economic history measures <strong>the long arc of wealth, work and trade</strong> — and asks
    the biggest question in the field: why did sustained growth start only ~250 years ago, after
    100,000 years of stagnation? The answer involves coal, colonies, institutions, science and
    luck — and its stakes include whether the growth can continue and be shared.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the arc: Malthusian trap, industrial breakout, booms, globalisation" widget="equationStep" config={econhistCfg} />

  <h2>The debates that define the field</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Great Divergence:</strong> when did Europe pull ahead? Pomeranz argues as late as 1800 (coal + New World resources as luck); others point to earlier institutional advantages (property rights, patent systems, scientific culture). The measurement itself (real wages, GDP estimates) is contested.</li>
    <li><strong>Slavery and capitalism:</strong> how much did Atlantic slavery finance industrialisation? Williams' thesis (1944) vs more recent quantifications — the debate remains live and politically charged.</li>
    <li><strong>Crisis cycles:</strong> Tulip mania (1637), South Sea (1720), 1873, 1929, 2008 — the anatomy of financial crashes is a repeated pattern this field tracks with increasing data (Reinhart & Rogoff's 800 years of crises).</li>
    <li><strong>Standards of living:</strong> real wages, height (nutrition proxy), life expectancy — the data that converts "growth" into human experience. The Industrial Revolution initially worsened many workers' lives ("the pessimist case") before delivering.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Maddison Project historical GDP data; Pomeranz, The Great Divergence; Allen, The Industrial Revolution in Global Perspective; Reinhart & Rogoff, This Time Is Different.</p>
</TopicPage>
